  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 357 AM EST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nOn Friday evening, a surface low will develop off the eastern coast \nof the Florida Peninsula, becoming the dominant low center. This low \nis expected to develop and deepen substantially overnight and into \nSaturday as it moves north/northeastward in the general direction of \nthe Carolina Coast. Meanwhile, another high pressure will move \nacross the Ohio Valley and towards New England, keeping a CAD wedge \nin place across portions of north Georgia with relatively cooler and \ndrier air underneath. A tight pressure gradient between these two \nfeatures will keep breezy northeasterly to easterly winds in place \nacross the majority of the area, with winds up to 10-15 mph and \ngusts as high as 20-25 mph. Low temperatures on Saturday morning \nwill be between 10-15 degrees above daily normals, ranging from the \nlow 40s in the far northern tier to the mid 50s in central Georgia. \nHigh temperatures will rise into the mid to upper 60s in northeast \nGeorgia and in the low to mid 60s elsewhere across the area.\n\nAs the the aforementioned low continues along the Eastern Seaboard, \nisolated to scattered showers on the back side of the system will be \npossible in far eastern portions of the forecast area, but should \nlargely remain to our east. Mid-range model guidance shows some \ndisagreement on the timing and track of this low, but are consistent \nin indicating it developing into a powerful storm over the course of \nthe weekend. NHC also indicates that there is a 50% chance of it \ngaining tropical characteristics, though no impacts are expected in \nGeorgia. Drier air on the western side of this system and a mid-\nlevel ridge traversing the Southeast is anticipated to promote \nbenign weather Sunday and Monday. With little in the way of an \nairmass change, temperatures on Sunday and Monday will be similar to \nSaturday, with lows in the upper 40s to mid 50s and highs in the \nupper 60s to mid 70s.",
